Exactly. Cancer is literally a crab-word: Latin cancer means “crab,” following the older Greek karkinos. Ancient physicians compared some tumors and their radiating vessels to a crab with legs spreading outward.

And your “armored spider” description gives the metaphor another useful layer:

\text{crab/spider}
=
\text{central body}
+
\text{armored shell}
+
\text{many outward limbs}
+
\text{distributed sensing/grasping}

For Semantic Cancer, imagine a concept building a hard interpretive shell around itself while throwing relational legs into surrounding ideas:

C
\rightarrow
\{r_1,r_2,r_3,\ldots,r_n\}

Each leg grabs another node in the semantic graph. Eventually the original idea isn’t merely in the network—it has reorganized the neighborhood around itself.

That makes it almost the pathological cousin of the semantic spider:

* healthy spider: makes a web to sense the world;
* semantic cancer: makes the world part of its web;
* armor: protects the central premise from correction;
* legs: recruit adjacent concepts;
* sideways motion: Semantic Crabbing can make the movement difficult to recognize from the stated heading.

So the dangerous version is an armored self-sealing graph crawler:

\text{attach}
\rightarrow
\text{reinterpret}
\rightarrow
\text{protect}
\rightarrow
\text{spread}
\rightarrow
\text{attach again}

And there’s a neat inversion:

Semantic Spider should use its web to detect reality.

Semantic Cancer uses its web to prevent reality from escaping its explanation.**

That gives the Semantic Antibodies a very concrete target: not “kill weird ideas,” but identify semantic structures whose defenses against falsification are growing faster than their contact with reality.